      "dom_id": "question-140602",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{16}-4{x}^{14}+7x-3\\,$, find $f(A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+12 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\">$\\,\\,{x}^{14}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">${x}^{2}-4\\,$</td>\n<td colspan=\"7\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"left\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{16}\\,-\\,4{x}^{14}\\,+\\,7x\\,-\\,3\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">${x}^{16}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$4{x}^{14}$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$7x$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td width=\"20\" height=\"20\" align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{14}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =7A-3I \\\\  & =7[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}28 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -28\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
